  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms look to \nfinish out the weekend as a front passes through. \n\nAdded some light showers and sprinkles in across central and western \nMD this evening as spotty activity drops south from central and \nwestern PA. Outside of these showers and sprinkles, is increased low \nand mid level cloudiness. This is in association with a squeeze play \nbetween fronts with moisture advection increasing from the south and \neast and a cold front slowly dropping down from the Great Lakes \nregion. Low and mid level clouds will remain abundant overnight with \nsome filtered breaks the further south you get toward the I-64 \ncorridor. Most will remain dry overnight, but with moisture \nadvection increasing over a shallow cool layer aloft expect humidity \nvalues to gradually climb and spotty showers likely developing over \nthe mountains heading into Sunday morning. This shower activity will \nbe scattered in nature and in association with the warm front and \nlead piece of shortwave energy ahead of the front that is currently \nlocated back across the eastern Great Lakes and Ohio River Valley. \nThe first round of showers should exit the area around midday with a \nsecondary area of showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms working \nthrough the region Sunday afternoon and evening as the cold front \ndrops through. Current hi-res guidance has slowed the front up a bit \nwhile dissipating/washing it out over the region Sunday evening into \nSunday night. Deterministic synoptic guidance has a similar \nsolution while keeping a ripple of low pressure riding along the\nboundary Sunday evening into Sunday night forcing it south. \nWith that said, severe weather does not appear to be a concern \nalthough a few thunderstorm cannot be ruled out over central VA,\nsouthern MD, and the northern neck of VA where some breaks of \nsun may filter through. Any of these storms will be capable of \nlocally gusty winds, heavy rainfall, and lightning. \n\nThe front will eventually shunt southward heading into Monday \nmorning although on and off shower activity will persists into \nthe new workweek given increased onshore flow. Expect a \nnoticeable cooling trend beginning Monday in the wake of the \ncold front late Sunday night. Highs Sunday will push into the \nupper 70s and low 80s (low 70s mountains) with overnight lows in\nthe upper 50s and mid 60s. Highs Monday will struggle to get \nout of the upper 60s and low 70s as more of a wedging pattern \nsets up with increasing onshore flow. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily shower chances and cooler temperatures persists through \nmidweek with drier conditions for the upcoming weekend. \n\nMid-level moisture overhead will coincide with an area of low \npressure just off the East Coast tracking north on Wednesday. Weak \ninstability at the surface will suppress thunderstorm activity for \nthe most part, but expect rain showers to continue through Wednesday \nand Thursday as shortwave disturbances track across the Mid- \nAtlantic. High temperatures continue to range in the 60s as \nthese disturbances move through. \n\nAs the coastal low skirts the Mid-Atlantic coastline, winds will \nbe elevated Wednesday and Thursday with wind gusts between 20-30 MPH \npossible, especially along the Chesapeake Bay. Winds could \npotentially be higher over the waters, and this onshore flow \ncombined with stronger tidal influence could increase coastal flood \nrisk during this period. Refer to the coastal flooding section below \nfor more details. \n\nAs this system tracks north towards the end of the week, winds along \nthe coastline should gradually decrease. Mid-level dry air moves in \noverhead from the Midwest, which should lead to diminishing rainfall \npotential heading into the weekend.",
